{"graph_chars": "Meet Alphonso. He has ten dollars a week. Burgers cost two dollars. Bus tickets cost fifty cents. If he spends everything on burgers, he can buy five, and zero tickets. If he spends everything on tickets, he can buy twenty, and zero burgers. Connect those points and you have his budget constraint: every combination that costs exactly ten dollars. Point A is five burgers. Point D is two burgers and twelve tickets. Point F is twenty tickets. The slope is negative zero point two five. Give up one burger, gain four tickets. The opportunity cost of a burger is four bus tickets, not merely two dollars. Economists think in what you forgo. Most real choices are not all-or-nothing. They are marginal: a little more or a little less. Utility is the satisfaction a good provides. The law of diminishing marginal utility says the first burger, on a hungry morning, beats the fifth burger that afternoon. A rational consumer buys another unit only while its marginal utility is at least as large as its opportunity cost. Alphonso will not buy twenty tickets. Sunk costs are costs already incurred and unrecoverable. Selena pays eight dollars for a movie, knows after thirty minutes that it is terrible, and should ignore the ticket. The money is gone. Staying still costs ninety minutes of her time. Forget the past. Decide on the margin.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0624], [0.0624, 0.1248], [0.1248, 0.1872], [0.1872, 0.2497], [0.2497, 0.3121], [0.3121, 0.3745], [0.3745, 0.4369], [0.4369, 0.4993], [0.4993, 0.5617], [0.5617, 0.6241], [0.6241, 0.6866], [0.6866, 0.749], [0.749, 0.8114], [0.8114, 0.8738], [0.8738, 0.9362], [0.9362, 0.9986], [0.9986, 1.061], [1.061, 1.1234], [1.1234, 1.1859], [1.1859, 1.2483], [1.2483, 1.3107], [1.3107, 1.3731], [1.3731, 1.4355], [1.4355, 1.4979], [1.4979, 1.5603], [1.5603, 1.6228], [1.6228, 1.6852], [1.6852, 1.7476], [1.7476, 1.81], [1.81, 1.8724], [1.8724, 1.9348], [1.9348, 1.9972], [1.9972, 2.0597], [2.0597, 2.1221], [2.1221, 2.1845], [2.1845, 2.